        <h2>Mimi Ọnụọha</h2>
        <p class="muted">Writer / researcher • data + power • “missing datasets” as a way to see who gets ignored</p>

        <p>
          I picked Mimi Ọnụọha because she flips a common “data mindset” upside down. Instead of only asking what a system measures,
          she asks what it <em>doesn’t</em> measure—and who pays the price for those blanks. In a course about Code &amp; Power, that
          feels like a practical way to spot how inequality becomes invisible.
        </p>

        <p>
          In her work on “missing datasets,” she describes blank spots in otherwise data-saturated spaces: areas where data
          <em>should</em> exist but doesn’t, and where that absence often correlates with vulnerability and unequal power.
          That lens connects directly to intersectionality: the people most likely to be missing from datasets are often those at
          multiple intersections (race/gender/class/disability/immigration status), where harms stack but documentation is weakest.
        </p>

        <h3>Why this connects to “Code &amp; Power”</h3>
        <ul class="list">
          <li><strong>Absence is political:</strong> what isn’t tracked can reveal whose suffering is “not legible” to institutions.</li>
          <li><strong>Intersectional harms:</strong> “one-size-fits-all” data hides compounded experiences across identities.</li>
          <li><strong>Accountability:</strong> a “neutral” model can’t be fair if the problem space is missing whole groups or outcomes.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Links</h3>
        <ul class="sources">
          <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/MimiOnuoha/missing-datasets" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
